---
export const prerender = false;
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { resolveLang } from '../lib/i18n';

const lang = resolveLang(Astro);

const copy = {
  zh: {
    title: '塔罗 × 戏剧治疗：自我探索',
    tagline: '创造性过程 · 图像而非预言',
    description: 'Inspire 如何融合塔罗图像与戏剧治疗实践，支持自我探索——不为占卜，而为成长与改变。',
    intro: 'Inspire 融合塔罗与戏剧治疗这两种开放性的实践，支持个人成长与创造性的自我探索。我们关注的是塔罗的图像——不为占卜，而是作为一面镜子，帮助你发现洞见、探索新的可能，并在事业、关系、身心健康与个人目标这些重要的人生领域里，找到改变的路径。',
    s1: '一句话概括',
    s1body: '塔罗提供图像，戏剧治疗提供走进图像的方法。牌面是一座原型场景的画廊，而戏剧治疗的练习把静止的画面，变成对真实生活的排练。',
    s2: '两种实践各带来什么',
    s2tarotTitle: '塔罗带来图像',
    s2tarot: '一套现成的原型情境语言：开始（愚人）、崩塌与重建（高塔）、滋养（女皇）、选择（恋人）、转折（命运之轮）。一个牌阵把几张牌并排放置，形成故事骨架：处境 → 阻碍 → 资源。而图像的模糊性恰恰邀请投射——这正是关键所在。',
    s2dramaTitle: '戏剧治疗带来舞台方法',
    s2drama: '让图像动起来的技术：投射式观看、身体化、角色扮演与角色互换、即兴、讲故事，以及一个郑重的"去角色"收尾，把洞见桥接回日常生活。没有观众，没有表演评判：这一切只为你而发生。',
    s3: '四个共通的机制',
    m1Title: '投射——牌是镜子',
    m1: '你在画面中注意到的东西（"她看起来被困住了""他正要跳下去"），会被当作关于你的资料，而不是关于牌的资料——这与戏剧治疗使用面具、沙盘、玩偶的机制相同。',
    m2Title: '原型遇上角色',
    m2: '戏剧治疗的角色理论认为，人生是通过一系列角色来演出的。大阿卡纳正是这样的角色库：牌为你命名一个你正在经历、已经失去或正在成长进入的角色，并让你把它穿上身。',
    m3Title: '审美距离',
    m3: '说"高塔"比说"我的离婚"安全。牌面是一层保护性的隐喻缓冲——近到能感受，远到能承受——而整个工作就在"谈论图像"与"成为图像"之间移动。',
    m4Title: '从图像到故事',
    m4: '一个牌阵本身就是故事的草稿。戏剧治疗把它变成被演出的故事：牌阵成为剧本，即兴成为排练，一张"命定"的图像变成一个有其他可能的场景。',
    s4: '一次探索如何进行',
    step1Title: '抽下图像',
    step1: '围绕一个真实的问题选牌——事业、关系、身心健康或一个目标。不做任何预测；图像只是起点画框。',
    step2Title: '投射式观看',
    step2: '只问开放的问题：这画面的情绪是什么？一秒之前发生了什么？接下来会发生什么？每个答案都是你自己的素材。',
    step3Title: '走进图像',
    step3: '摆出牌中人物的姿态，像他们那样走动，说出第一句台词。静止的牌面变成舞台——这是塔罗交给戏剧治疗的枢纽时刻。',
    step4Title: '角色与对话',
    step4: '以人物的口吻说话，进行角色互换（你 ↔ 牌，或两张牌互相对话），即兴演出"下一场"。新的行为被排练出来，而不只是被讨论。',
    step5Title: '反思与去角色',
    step5: '退回自己，说出浮现的东西，并正式离开角色——你带走的是洞见，不是戏服。',
    step6Title: '桥接行动',
    step6: '排练变成真实世界的小实验：一场要进行的对话、一条要设立的边界、一个具体的下一步。',
    s5: '探索的落点',
    thArea: '人生领域',
    thImagery: '典型图像',
    thRehearse: '舞台上排练什么',
    row1: ['事业', '战车、星币八、皇帝', '一次谈判、一个领导者姿态、守住一条底线'],
    row2: ['关系', '恋人、圣杯二、宝剑五', '角色互换——诚实地替对方把理由说一遍'],
    row3: ['身心健康', '节制、星星、宝剑四', '用身体"雕塑"平衡；搭建一幕真正的休息'],
    row4: ['个人目标', '愚人、高塔、命运之轮', '为冒险的第一步——连同它的失败方式——做一次排练'],
    s6: '为什么"不为占卜"如此重要',
    h1Title: '主动权回到你手中。',
    h1Body: '牌不决定你的未来——是你告诉牌你看见了什么。你的回应，而不是解牌者的解释，才是权威文本。',
    h2Title: '用排练代替被动等待。',
    h2Body: '没有需要等待的未来，只有现在就可以练习的一幕。改变通过身体里的重复发生，而不是通过接收一条讯息。',
    h3Title: '方法本身是诚实的。',
    h3Body: '投射、身体化与排练，是戏剧治疗面对任何素材都会使用的机制。塔罗牌只是提供了一套异常丰富的图像——不需要任何神秘主义。',
    cta: '感受这个过程最自然的方式，就是亲自试一次。带着一个真正重要的问题开始解读——把出现的牌当作舞台，而不是判决。',
    ctaButton: '开始一次解读',
  },
  en: {
    title: 'Tarot & Drama Therapy for Self Exploration',
    tagline: 'A creative process · images, not predictions',
    description: 'How Inspire blends tarot imagery with drama therapy practices for insight, possibility, and change — not divination.',
    intro: 'Inspire is a creative process that blends the expansive practices of tarot and drama therapy to support personal growth and creative self-exploration. We use tarot\u2019s imagery — not for divination — as a tool to uncover insights, explore new possibilities, and navigate pathways for change in key areas of life: career, relationships, well-being, and personal goals.',
    s1: 'One idea holds it together',
    s1body: 'Tarot supplies the image, and drama therapy supplies the way of stepping into it. The cards are a gallery of archetypal scenes; the drama therapy practices turn a static picture into a rehearsal for real life.',
    s2: 'What each practice brings',
    s2tarotTitle: 'Tarot brings the image',
    s2tarot: 'A ready-made vocabulary of archetypal situations: beginnings (the Fool), collapse and rebuilding (the Tower), care (the Empress), choice (the Lovers), turning points (the Wheel). A spread lays several side by side, forming a story skeleton: situation → obstacle → resource. And because the images are ambiguous, they invite projection — which is exactly the point.',
    s2dramaTitle: 'Drama therapy brings the stagecraft',
    s2drama: 'Techniques for making an image move: projective looking, embodiment, role-play and role reversal, improvisation, storytelling — plus a deliberate cool-down that de-roles you and bridges insight back into daily life. No audience, no performance: the work exists for you.',
    s3: 'Four shared mechanisms',
    m1Title: 'Projection — the card as mirror',
    m1: 'What you notice in the picture ("she looks trapped", "he is about to leap") is treated as data about you, not about the card — the same mechanism drama therapy uses with masks, sand trays, and dolls.',
    m2Title: 'Archetype meets role',
    m2: 'Drama therapy\u2019s role theory says a life is played through a repertoire of roles. The arcana are precisely such roles: the card names one you are in, have lost, or are growing into — and lets you try it on.',
    m3Title: 'Aesthetic distance',
    m3: 'Saying "the Tower" is safer than saying "my divorce." The card is a protective metaphorical buffer — close enough to feel, far enough to survive — and the work moves between talking about the image and being the image.',
    m4Title: 'Story from images',
    m4: 'A spread is already a narrative draft. Drama therapy turns it into acted story: the spread becomes the script, improvisation becomes rehearsal, and a fixed image becomes a scene with alternatives.',
    s4: 'How a session works',
    step1Title: 'Draw the image',
    step1: 'Choose a card around a real question — career, relationship, well-being, a goal. Nothing is predicted; the image is a starting frame.',
    step2Title: 'Projective looking',
    step2: 'Open questions only: What is the mood here? What happened a second before? What happens next? Every answer is your own material.',
    step3Title: 'Step into the image',
    step3: 'Take the figure\u2019s posture, walk as them, speak a first line. The static card becomes a stage — the hinge where tarot hands over to drama therapy.',
    step4Title: 'Role work & dialogue',
    step4: 'Speak as the figure, reverse roles (you ↔ the card, or two cards in conversation), improvise the "scene after." New behavior gets rehearsed instead of only discussed.',
    step5Title: 'Reflect & de-role',
    step5: 'Step back out, name what surfaced, and formally leave the role — you keep the insight, not the costume.',
    step6Title: 'Bridge to action',
    step6: 'The rehearsal becomes a real-world experiment: a conversation to have, a boundary to set, one concrete next step.',
    s5: 'Where the work lands',
    thArea: 'Life area',
    thImagery: 'Typical imagery',
    thRehearse: 'What gets rehearsed',
    row1: ['Career', 'The Chariot, Eight of Pentacles, The Emperor', 'A negotiation, a leadership stance, holding a limit'],
    row2: ['Relationships', 'The Lovers, Two of Cups, Five of Swords', 'Role reversal — arguing the other person\u2019s case honestly'],
    row3: ['Well-being', 'Temperance, The Star, Four of Swords', 'Sculpting balance in the body; building a scene of genuine rest'],
    row4: ['Personal goals', 'The Fool, The Tower, Wheel of Fortune', 'Rehearsing the risky first step — and its failure mode'],
    s6: 'Why "not for divination" matters',
    h1Title: 'Agency moves to you.',
    h1Body: 'The card doesn\u2019t decide your future — you tell the card what you see. Your response, not a reader\u2019s interpretation, is the authoritative text.',
    h2Title: 'Rehearsal instead of passivity.',
    h2Body: 'There is no future to wait for; there is a scene to practice now. Change happens through repetition in the body, not through receiving a message.',
    h3Title: 'An honest method.',
    h3Body: 'Projection, embodiment, and rehearsal are mechanisms drama therapy already uses with any prompt material. The deck simply offers an unusually rich set of images — nothing mystical is required.',
    cta: 'The most natural way to feel this process is to try it. Start a reading with a question that matters — and treat the cards that appear as a stage, not a verdict.',
    ctaButton: 'Start a reading',
  },
} as const;

const t = copy[lang];
const steps = [
  { title: t.step1Title, body: t.step1 },
  { title: t.step2Title, body: t.step2 },
  { title: t.step3Title, body: t.step3 },
  { title: t.step4Title, body: t.step4 },
  { title: t.step5Title, body: t.step5 },
  { title: t.step6Title, body: t.step6 },
];
const mechanisms = [
  { title: t.m1Title, body: t.m1 },
  { title: t.m2Title, body: t.m2 },
  { title: t.m3Title, body: t.m3 },
  { title: t.m4Title, body: t.m4 },
];
const rows = [t.row1, t.row2, t.row3, t.row4];
const hinge = [
  { title: t.h1Title, body: t.h1Body },
  { title: t.h2Title, body: t.h2Body },
  { title: t.h3Title, body: t.h3Body },
];
---
<Layout title={t.title} description={t.description} lang={lang}>
  <div class="policy-page">
    <div class="policy-head">
      <img src="/logo.webp" alt="Inspire" class="site-logo" width="1024" height="1024" />
      <h1>{t.title}</h1>
      <p class="policy-updated">{t.tagline}</p>
    </div>

    <p class="policy-intro">{t.intro}</p>

    <section class="policy-section">
      <h2>{t.s1}</h2>
      <p class="dt-thesis">{t.s1body}</p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-section">
      <h2>{t.s2}</h2>
      <div class="dt-grid">
        <div class="dt-card">
          <h3>{t.s2tarotTitle}</h3>
          <p>{t.s2tarot}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="dt-card">
          <h3>{t.s2dramaTitle}</h3>
          <p>{t.s2drama}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-section">
      <h2>{t.s3}</h2>
      <div class="dt-grid">
        {mechanisms.map((m, i) => (
          <div class="dt-card">
            <h3><span class="dt-num">{i + 1}</span>{m.title}</h3>
            <p>{m.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-section">
      <h2>{t.s4}</h2>
      <ol class="dt-steps">
        {steps.map((s, i) => (
          <li>
            <span class="dt-step-n">{i + 1}</span>
            <div class="dt-step-body">
              <h3>{s.title}</h3>
              <p>{s.body}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-section">
      <h2>{t.s5}</h2>
      <table class="policy-table">
        <tr>
          <th>{t.thArea}</th>
          <th>{t.thImagery}</th>
          <th>{t.thRehearse}</th>
        </tr>
        {rows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="dt-area">{row[0]}</td>
            <td>{row[1]}</td>
            <td>{row[2]}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </table>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-section">
      <h2>{t.s6}</h2>
      <ul class="dt-hinge">
        {hinge.map((h) => (
          <li><strong>{h.title}</strong> {h.body}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="upsell-cta">
      <p class="upsell-message">{t.cta}</p>
      <a class="upsell-button" href="/">{t.ctaButton}</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .dt-thesis {
    background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--gold); border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 18px 20px; line-height: 1.75; color: var(--text); font-size: 15px;
  }
  .dt-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 14px; }
  .dt-card {
    background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--panel-border);
    border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px;
  }
  .dt-card h3 { color: var(--gold); font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .dt-card p { color: var(--text); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.7; margin: 0; }
  .dt-num {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    width: 22px; height: 22px; border-radius: 50%; margin-right: 8px;
    background: var(--accent); color: #fff; font-size: 12px; vertical-align: 1px;
  }
  .dt-steps { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .dt-steps li { display: flex; gap: 14px; padding: 12px 0; border-bottom: 1px dashed var(--panel-border); }
  .dt-steps li:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .dt-step-n {
    flex: 0 0 30px; height: 30px; border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--gold); color: #1a1a1a; font-weight: bold; font-size: 14px;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  }
  .dt-step-body h3 { margin: 2px 0 4px; font-size: 15px; color: var(--text); }
  .dt-step-body p { margin: 0; font-size: 14px; color: var(--text-dim); line-height: 1.65; }
  .dt-area { color: var(--gold); }
  .dt-hinge { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .dt-hinge li { position: relative; padding: 8px 0 8px 24px; font-size: 14px; color: var(--text); line-height: 1.7; }
  .dt-hinge li::before {
    content: ""; position: absolute; left: 4px; top: 17px;
    width: 8px; height: 8px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--gold);
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .dt-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
